g(x)=0,\\,{lim}_{x\\to {2}^{+}}g(x)=1\n$$\n\n- The function $h(x)$ is not piecewise continuous over $x\\in [0,\\infty ).$ Consider the closed subinterval $x\\in [0,2\\pi ].$ Notice that the one-sided limits at $x=\\pi $ are infinite:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {\\pi }^{-}}h(x)=+\\infty ,\\,{lim}_{x\\to {\\pi }^{+}}h(x)=-\\infty .\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$f$ and $g$ only.\"" 13194139535424 true] [17592186317806 1020 #edb/ref 17592186348580 13194139535424 true] [17592186317806 1020 #edb/ref 17592186348587 13194139535424 true] [17592186348566 1017 #edb/ref 17592186348567 13194139535424 true] [17592186348566 1089 "q-341642" 13194139535424 true] [17592186348566 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535424 true] [17592186348566 1100 "Which of the following functions are piecewise continuous on $x\\in [0,\\infty ).$\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\sqrt{x+1},\\,g(x)={\\begin{aligned}1,\\, & ⌈x⌉\\text{ is even} \\\\ -1,\\, & ⌈x⌉\\text{ is odd},\\end{aligned}\\,h(x)=u(x-1)+u\\,(x-\\frac{5}{2})+u(x-4).\n$$\n\nNote that $u(x)$ is the unit step function, and $⌈x⌉$ is the ceiling function." 13194139535424 true] [17592186348566 1113 #uuid "4689ffaf-d7a6-5ea0-9161-3ec67ff19338" 13194139535424 true] [17592186348566 1145 "Suppose $I⊆R.$ A function $f:I\\to R$ is piecewise continuous on $I$ if it is piecewise continuous on every closed and bounded subinterval $[a,b]⊂I.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each function.\n\n- The function $f(x)$ is piecewise continuous over $x\\in [0,\\infty ).$ The function is continuous over $x\\in [0,\\infty ).$\n\n- The function $g(x)$ is piecewise continuous over $x\\in [0,\\infty ).$ The function has jump discontinuities at $x=0,1,2,…,$ and any closed interval $[a,b]⊂[0,\\infty )$ will contain finitely many discontinuities.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14064583/assets/d5a497337426dad07096702a3e211f525a959add8cb1fbaaef05fccac26d634b.png)\n\n- The function $h(x)$ is piecewise continuous over $x\\in [0,\\infty )$ since it is continuous over $x\\in [0,\\infty )$ except at the points $x=1,$ $x=\\frac{5}{2},$ and $x=4,$ where the one-sided limits exist and are finite:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}h(x)=0,\\,{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}h(x)=1\n$$\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to 5/{2}^{-}}h(x)=1,\\,{lim}_{x\\to 5/{2}^{+}}h(x)=2\n$$\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {4}^{-}}h(x)=2,\\,{lim}_{x\\to {4}^{+}}h(x)=3\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$f,g,$ and $h.$\"" 13194139535424 true] [17592186348567 1000 #edb/ref 17592186348568 13194139535424 true] [17592186348567 1000 #edb/ref 17592186348569 13194139535424 true] [17592186348567 1000 #edb/ref 17592186348570 13194139535424 true] [17592186348567 1000 #edb/ref 17592186348571 13194139535424 true] [17592186348567 1000 #edb/ref 17592186348572 13194139535424 true] [17592186348567 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535424 true] [17592186348567 1041 "selection" 13194139535424 true] [17592186348567 1104 #edb/ref 17592186348569 13194139535424 true] [17592186348567 1110 #uuid "9780f17b-d59d-5fb5-987b-1cb503eb4e2f" 13194139535424 true] [17592186348568 1034 "$h$ only" 13194139535424 true] [17592186348568 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535424 true] [17592186348568 1111 #uuid "c6b1a7a3-8afa-5e0e-ac81-bc341c292cba" 13194139535424 true] [17592186348569 1034 "$f,g,$ and $h$" 13194139535424 true] [17592186348569 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535424 true] [17592186348569 1111 #uuid "7bafdee7-112c-5f2d-b2a6-51e971cbc1dd" 13194139535424 true] [17592186348570 1034 "$g$ only" 13194139535424 true] [17592186348570 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535424 true] [17592186348570 1111 #uuid "e1ac55ce-3f38-521c-a98a-2b1dfe369b40" 13194139535424 true] [17592186348571 1034 "$g$ and $h$ only" 13194139535424 true] [17592186348571 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535424 true] [17592186348571 1111 #uuid "4af00528-014e-5750-989f-5a966ddbbecf" 13194139535424 true] [17592186348572 1034 "$f$ and $h$ only" 13194139535424 true] [17592186348572 1103 